d | FOMC statement 2026-07-29 |\n| Policy stance | Neutral-to-accommodative per natural-rate estimates | US News, 2026-08-17 |\n| DXY | ~98.9, weak/down | MarketWatch 2026-08-25 |\n| IG credit spread (OAS) | 0.81% | FRED, 2026-08-24 |\n| Inflation | [Unconfirmed] \u2014 not retrieved within budget | \u2014 |\n| GDP cycle | [Unconfirmed] late-expansion assumed | \u2014 |\n\n**Regime probabilities:**\n| Regime | Probability | Implication |\n|---|---|---|\n| Reflation (High rates + Growth) | 40% | Favors value/cyclicals; hurts long-duration multiple |\n| Goldilocks (Low rates + Growth) | 25% | Favors quality software |\n| Stagflation (High rates + Slow growth) | 20% | Favors defensives/quality |\n| Recession (Low rates + Slow growth) | 15% | Favors long-duration; depresses current revenues |\n\n**ROP macro sensitivity (scale -3 to +3, negative = headwind):**\n- Interest-rate sensitivity: **-1** (multiple sensitive, but low capital intensity)\n- Dollar sensitivity: **-0.5** (~20-25% international [Assumption])\n- Liquidity sensitivity: **+1** (recurring software benefits from stable liquidity)\n- Economic-cycle sensitivity: **-1** (defensive vertical software)\n- Commodity sensitivity: **-2** (not commodity-exposed)\n\n**Expected macro impact:** Slightly negative to neutral. High real rates pressure the software multiple; high quality and recurring revenue offset. Macro alignment: **Neutral** \u2014 no conviction adjustment.\n\n---\n\n## SECTION 1.5. BASE RATE & HISTORICAL PATTERN\n\n**Base rates applied [Estimate]:**\n- Quality Compounder survival over 5 years: ~90%+\n- Option/Platform transition survival: 40\u201360% (not applicable)\n- Large M&A ROIC > WACC: 25\u201335% (relevant due to Roper\u2019s M&A model)\n- Vertical-software recurring model producing positive FCF within 5 years: ~85%\n\n**Deviation from base rate:** ROP\u2019s expected success is consistent with, not above, base rates. Company-specific justifications:\n1. 12-year track record of >28% operating margins and >100% FCF conversion [Actual].\n2. Disciplined divestiture strategy completed (sale of industrial businesses to CD&R) [Actual].\n3. Niche vertical-software model with high switching costs, demonstrated by stable organic growth >4% [Estimate].\n\n**Base-rate shock check:** Success probability is not >1.5x base rate; no red-flag requirement.\n\n---\n\n## SECTION 2. BUSINESS QUALITY ASSESSMENT\n\n**2-1. Business understanding (3 sentences):**\nRoper acquires and operates a diversified portfolio of mission-critical niche vertical-software businesses (healthcare, transportation, construction, public safety). It generates highly recurring revenue, ~70% gross margin, and consistently converts 95-100% of operating income into free cash flow. After the CD&R industrial divestiture, Roper is effectively a pure-play vertical-software compounder.\n\n**Understandability:** Medium (diversified portfolio, but model is simple).\n\n**2-2. Moat score:**\n| Moat type | Score (0-3) | Direction |\n|---|---|---|\n| Intangible assets | 2 | Stable |\n| Switching costs | 3 | Stable |\n| Network effects | 1 | Stable |\n| Cost advantage | 2 | Stable |\n| Economies of scale | 2 | Stable |\n| Regulatory barriers | 1 | Stable |\n| Data moat | 2 | Narrowing (AI entrants) |\n| **Total** | **13/21** | **Wide moat, stable** |\n\n**2-3. Lynch classification:** **Stalwart** \u2014 revenue CAGR 10.2% trailing 5-year [Actual], organic growth ~5-6% [Estimate]. Not a hyper-grower; not a pure cyclical.\n\n**2-4. Financial strength scorecard [Actual unless tagged]:**\n| Metric | Value |\n|---|---|\n| Revenue CAGR, 3-yr (FY22-FY25) | 13.7% [Arithmetic] |\n| Revenue growth, last TTM YoY | ~8.5% [Arithmetic] |\n| Operating margin, FY2025 | 28.3% [Actual] |\n| Operating margin, 12-yr median | 28.0% [Actual] |\n| ROE (vendor) | 13.1% [Estimate] |\n| FCF margin, TTM | 31.5% [Arithmetic] |\n| Net debt/EBITDA | ~2.9x [Estimate] |\n| Interest coverage | ~5x [Estimate, FY24-25 interest unavailable] |\n\n**Judgment: Excellent.**\n\n**2-5. Capital allocation scorecard (0-15):**\n| Item | Score | Notes |\n|---|---|---|\n| Buyback efficiency | 1 | No buyback in FY2025 (+0.2% net issuance) [Actual]; reported $3.2B buyback in 2026 [Unconfirmed] |\n| M&A track record | 3 | Long track record; CD&R divestiture disciplined [Actual] |\n| Capex efficiency | 3 | Minimal capex; high incremental ROIC [Estimate] |\n| Rationality of mix | 3 | Matching cash flows to M&A/buyback [Estimate] |\n| Guidance accuracy | 2 | FY2026 DEPS guidance raised twice [Actual] |\n| **Total** | **12/15** | Plus insider ownership <1%, no net insider buying [Actual] -> no bonus |\n| **Capital allocation grade** | **Good-to-Excellent** | |\n\n---\n\n## SECTION 2.5. INTANGIBLES-ADJUSTED FINANCIALS\n\n**SBC-adjusted FCF [Arithmetic]:**\n- FY2025 FCF = $2.493B [Actual]\n- FY2025 SBC = $0.166B [Actual]\n- Adjusted FCF = **$2.327B**\n- TTM FCF = $2.612B [Actual]; less SBC ~$0.166B [Estimate] = **$2.446B**\n\n**Owner earnings (FY2025):**\n- Net income $1.536B [Actual]\n- + D&A $0.898B [Actual]\n- - Maintenance capex $0.047B [Actual]\n- - \u0394Working capital +$0.492B [Arithmetic]\n- = **$1.895B** (conservative)\n\n**Adjusted ROIC:** GAAP ROIC is low (~6-7%) because of acquisition-related goodwill. Adjusted for amortization and SBC, estimated ROIC ~10-12% [Estimate]. Segment/goodwill details are [Unconfirmed]; no reliable intangible-adjusted ROIC can be fully computed from pack.\n\n**Operating leases:** $0.234B FY2025 [Actual]; immaterial relative to gross debt.\n\n**Quality-of-earnings note:** Q2 2026 GAAP net income ($1.169B) includes non-recurring/minority investment and beneficial-interest effects [Unconfirmed]. TTM net income is not run-rate; TTM FCF remains cleaner.\n\n---\n\n## SECTION 3. VALUATION\n\n**Engine selected:** **Engine 1 \u2014 Traditional DCF + Multiples** (Archetype B, Quality Compounder). Reason: stable recurring revenue, high margin, low cyclicality, 12-year history.\n\n**Discount-rate derivation:**\n| Component | Value | Source |\n|---|---|---|\n| Risk-free rate | 4.71% | [Actual] FRED |\n| Beta | 0.737 | [Actual] vendor, unspecified window |\n| Equity risk premium | 5.0% | [Assumption] |\n| Cost of equity | 8.40% | CAPM |\n| Pre-tax cost of debt | 5.0% | [Assumption] |\n| After-tax cost of debt | 3.95% | 21% tax rate |\n| Equity weight | 81.8% | [Actual] market data |\n| Debt weight | 18.2% | [Actual] net debt $9.05B |\n| **WACC** | **7.6%** | For EV cross-check |\n\n**Forecast drivers \u2014 sourced:**\n| Year | Revenue ($B) | Growth | FCF margin | FCFE ($B) | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 2026 | 8.60 | +8.8% | 29.5% | 2.54 | H1 run-rate + raised guidance [Actual/Estimate] |\n| 2027 | 9.16 | +6.5% | 30.0% | 2.75 | Consensus forward rev +6.1% [Estimate] |\n| 2028 | 9.71 | +6.0% | 30.2% | 2.93 | Decay to mid-cycle [Assumption] |\n| 2029 | 10.24 | +5.5% | 30.4% | 3.11 | Decay [Assumption] |\n| 2030 | 10.76 | +5.0% | 30.5% | 3.28 | Decay [Assumption] |\n\n*Note:* FCF margin = trailing FCF margin 31.5% minus SBC ~2pp, with modest expansion [Estimate]. Post-guidance growth path is not company-issued; it is an [Assumption]. The valuation is deliberately stress-tested across scenarios.\n\n**DCF (base case):**\n- PV of 2026\u20132030 FCFE at 8.40%: **$11.4B**\n- Terminal growth 3.5% [Assumption]\n- Terminal value = $3.28 \u00d7 1.035 / (0.084 \u2212 0.035) = **$69.3B**\n- PV of terminal value = **$46.3B**\n- Equity value = **$57.7B**\n- Per share = **$583**\n\n**Multiples cross-check [Actual/Estimate]:**\n- Trailing P/E (GAAP) 16.6x; adjusted DEPS $22.15\u2013$22.30 FY2026 implies forward P/E ~18.6x\n- EV/EBITDA ~15.9x on FY2025 EBITDA $3.13B\n- P/FCF (TTM adjusted) ~16.7x\n\nSingle DCF at base assumptions suggests meaningful undervaluation; however, high discount-rate sensitivity requires a scenario distribution rather than a point estimate.\n\n---\n\n## SECTION 3.5. SECOND-ORDER EFFECTS\n\n**Competitive response:** If Roper succeeds with AI-enabled vertical SaaS, larger horizontal players (e.g., ServiceNow, Microsoft) and vertical-native startups will target adjacent workflows. Counter-impact: **-2** (moderate negative over time).\n\n**Reflexivity:** A low share price reduces acquisition currency and SBC attractiveness; a higher price increases M&A optionality. Roper\u2019s management has historically used cash, not stock, limiting reflexivity risk. Score: **+1** positive.\n\n**Customer evolution:** Early adopters in healthcare/transportation are sticky; mainstream penetration increases churn risk as low-end competitors emerge. LTV/CAC likely declines over time but remains favorable. Assumption: renewal rates >90% [Estimate].\n\n---\n\n## SECTION 4. SCENARIOS (BAYESIAN)\n\n| Scenario | Probability | Assumptions | Value/share |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Bull | 25% | FCF growth 9%, terminal g 4.0%, discount 8.2% | $620 |\n| Base | 50% | FCF growth 7%, terminal g 3.5%, discount 8.6% | $490 |\n| Bear | 25% | FCF growth 3%, terminal g 2.5%, discount 9.5% | $350 |\n\n**Expected price = 0.25\u00d7620 + 0.50\u00d7490 + 0.25\u00d7350 = $488**\n\n**50% CI:** $430\u2013$540 | **80% CI:** $340\u2013$650\n**Margin of safety = ($488 \u2212 $411.79) / $488 = 15.6% \u2192 Adequate**\n\n**Bayesian update:** Prior base rate on quality compounder success = ~90%. Bear scenario probability raised to 25% (vs. 15%) because of AI disruption and rising leverage risk. No additional observable update yet.\n\n---\n\n## SECTION 4.5. HORIZON ARBITRAGE\n\nCurrent market price implies roughly 3.0\u20133.5% perpetual FCF growth at an 8.4\u20139.0% required return (reverse DCF). My base-case horizon is 3\u20135 years operating edge plus a 3.5% terminal fade; market appears to be discounting a shorter/weaker growth runway by approximately **1.5\u20132 years**. A moderate arbitrage exists: **+1.5 years**.\n\n---\n\n## SECTION 5. CONVICTION & POSITION SIZING\n\n| Component | Score (0-3) |\n|---|---|\n| Business quality | 3 |\n| Valuation attractiveness | 2 |\n| Macro alignment | 2 |\n| Thesis clarity | 3 |\n| Execution traceability | 3 |\n| **Raw total** | **13/15** |\n\n**Adjustments:** Red-team logic gap <2 and crowding risk reduce conviction cap. **Final Conviction: High (11/15)**.\n\n**Sizing guideline:** High conviction max weight ~8%; crowding discount \u22121pp; **target weight 7%**.\n\n**Conviction decay:** Automatic \u22121 per 6 months absent confirming organic growth/renewal data.\n\n---\n\n## SECTION 5.5. BEHAVIORAL & POSITIONING\n\n| Indicator | Value | Signal |\n|---|---|---|\n| Short % of float | 6.25% | Moderately elevated; squeeze potential if earnings beat |\n| Shares short | 5.38M | ~5.9 days to cover |\n| MoM short change | \u22126.8% | Shorts covering; sentiment improving |\n| Institutional ownership | 106.8% | Very high; vulnerable to ETF/fund outflows |\n| Insider ownership | 0.39% | Low; no insider signal |\n| Options positioning | [Unconfirmed] | Not held |\n| **Crowding risk score** | **8/15** | Position size \u22121pp |\n\n**Sentiment:** Price $411 is below analyst mean target $440 and near lower half of 52-week range ($306\u2013$545). Bearish bias appears somewhat crowded.\n\n---\n\n## SECTION 6. PORTFOLIO FIT\n\nROP adds:\n- Low-beta exposure (0.737)\n- High FCF yield relative to market\n- Diversification away from industrial cyclicality\n- Correlation with tech growth lower than Nasdaq average\n\nA 7% position fits a diversified equity portfolio, replacing either mid-cap software or defensive staples.\n\n---\n\n## SECTION 6.5. KELLY SIZING\n\nUsing scenario distribution:\n- Expected value gain = +$76/share (+18.5%)\n- Bear-case loss = \u2212$62/share (\u221215.0%)\n- Probability-weighted edge/risk ratio \u2248 1.2x\n\nFull Kelly estimate \u2248 16\u201320% of liquid capital (sensitivity-driven). Applying 0.5x Kelly and the crowding adjustment reduces the target to **7\u20138%**. The gap between conviction-based sizing (8%) and Kelly-based sizing (7%) is <20pp, so no re-analysis