5 \u2014 KELLY SIZING\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\u2022 Estimated probability of reaching IV: 45% (Bayesian posterior).\n\u2022 Payoff ratio: upside to IV \u2248 $527.75, downside to bear \u2248 $339 (and to 52-wk low\n  $306 in tail). Average win = +$116; average loss = \u2212$73 (blended bear/tail).\n\u2022 Odds ratio b = 116/73 = 1.59.\n\u2022 Kelly fraction f* = p \u2212 (1\u2212p)/b = 0.45 \u2212 0.55/1.59 = 0.45 \u2212 0.346 = 10.4%.\n\u2022 Half-Kelly = 5.2%.\n\u2022 Layer 5 sizing (5%) vs Kelly (5.2%): gap = 0.2pp \u2192 well within the 20pp\n  re-analysis threshold. CONVERGED.\nFinal equity weight: 5.0%.\n\nSECTION 7 \u2014 RISKS & CATALYSTS\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nUpside catalysts:\n1. Organic growth re-accelerates to 6-7% (AI upsell, Deltek recovery) \u2014 the primary\n   KPI.\n2. Leverage paydown resumes (FCF $2.6B/yr); net debt/EBITDA back to <2.5x by 2027,\n   restoring M&A engine.\n3. Continued buybacks below $400 \u2014 every $1B at $380 removes ~2.6M shares (~2.7%).\n4. Rates fall 50-75bp; long-duration software re-rates 2-4 multiple points.\n\nDownside risks:\n1. Organic growth <3% for two quarters (AI disruption, budget cycles) \u2014 thesis broken.\n2. Net debt/EBITDA >4x if EBITDA dips or more debt-funded buybacks.\n3. M&A price inflation: CentralReach at 22x EBITDA sets a precedent for value\n   destruction.\n4. GAAP ROIC < WACC persists (5.7% vs 7.5%) \u2014 indicates goodwill-driven book value.\n5. Government/healthcare budget austerity hits Deltek and healthcare software.\n\nPre-mortem (mandatory): In 5 years, ROP is at $250 because: (1) organic growth fell\nfrom 5-6% to 2-3% as AI-native vertical tools replaced high-touch UI; (2) Roper had\nno dry powder (3.5x leverage) to acquire the disruptors; (3) the market de-rated\nquality compounders to 14x earnings. Early warning indicators: organic growth <3%\n2 quarters; recurring revenue growth <4%; renewal rates <85%; M&A deployment <$1B/yr;\nEBITDA margin <38%.\n\nSECTION 7.5 \u2014 CORRELATION-ADJUSTED RISK\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\u2022 Common bear triggers with the rest of a typical equity portfolio: a 100bp rise in\n  10Y yields, credit spread widening (M&A/leveraged model vulnerable), and software\n  multiple compression.\n\u2022 Tail correlation: ROP vs NASDAQ during risk-off: historically ~0.7 (2020, 2022)\n  [Estimate - not retrieved, assumed from beta and sector]; ROP will NOT decouple in\n  a real recession.\n\u2022 CVaR (95%, 1-year): \u221230% to \u221235% [Estimate], implying a tail price of ~$270\u2013290,\n  below the 52-wk low.\n\u2022 Portfolio-level: 5% position \u00d7 \u221232.5% CVaR = \u22121.6% portfolio impact in tail. Acceptable.\n\nSECTION 8 \u2014 MONITORING RULES\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nRe-evaluation triggers (any two within two quarters):\n\u2022 Organic revenue growth <3% (company-reported).\n\u2022 Recurring software revenue growth <5%.\n\u2022 Net debt/EBITDA >4.0x (company-reported).\n\u2022 Adjusted FCF margin ex-SBC <27%.\n\u2022 Buyback suspension or M&A guidance withdrawal.\n\u2022 Two consecutive quarters of adjusted DEPS guidance cuts.\nPositive update triggers (add to conviction/size):\n\u2022 Organic growth \u22656% for 2 quarters.\n\u2022 Deleveraging >$1.5B in 12 months.\n\u2022 Major AI-driven product wins disclosed on earnings calls.\n\nMonitoring cadence: quarterly earnings, monthly short interest, weekly price vs\n$380/$350 tranche levels.\n\nSECTION 9 \u2014 RED TEAM (ADVERSARIAL)\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nShort thesis (5-7 bullets):\n1. Organic growth has decelerated from ~8% to 5% and Deltek/government demand is\n   soft [Estimate - ts2.tech report on weak 2026 outlook].\n2. AI is the moat-killer: LLM copilots could erode