oding future IRRs. Impact: \u22121 [Assumption].\n\n3.5-2 Reflexivity: A weak Roper stock (down 24% from high) reduces management's\nacquisition currency (though deals are cash), weakens employee morale (SBC less\nvaluable), and invites activist attention. A strong stock would do the reverse.\nMagnitude: moderate. Current reflexivity is mildly negative.\n\n3.5-3 Customer behavior: Roper's niche vertical-software customers are\nmission-critical users with high switching costs; as penetration deepens, churn stays\nlow (vertical software renewal rates typically 88-93% [Estimate]) but new-customer\ngains slow. AI copilots could extend or disrupt this cycle \u2014 the key unknown.\n\n\nSECTION 4 \u2014 SCENARIOS (BAYESIAN)\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nScenario inputs derived from Sections 1.5 base rates, 3 (DCF), and 3.5 (second-order).\n\n| Scenario | Prob | Realization path | Key assumptions | IV |\n|----------|------|------------------|-----------------|----|\n| BEAR     | 25%  | Organic 4% \u2192 3.5%; M&A stalled at 3.3x leverage; AI disruption accelerates; FCF margin erodes to 28.5%; WACC 8.5%; TG 2.5% | DCF at WACC 8.5%, growth 4.5% \u2192 3.5%, TG 2.5% \u2192 IV $339 [Arithmetic] |\n| BASE     | 50%  | Organic 5-6% holds; M&A contributes 1.5-2.5% post-deleveraging; WACC 7.5%; TG 3.0%; FCF margin 30% | DCF as in Section 3 \u2192 IV $571 [Arithmetic] |\n| BULL     | 25%  | Organic 6-7%; AI becomes a tailwind/upsell driver; M&A re-accelerates in 2027; WACC 7.25%; TG 3.25%; FCF margin 30.5% | DCF at WACC 7.25%, growth 8% fading to 6%, TG 3.25% \u2192 IV $630 [Arithmetic] |\n\nExpected IV = 0.25\u00d7$339 + 0.50\u00d7$571 + 0.25\u00d7$630 = $527.75\nMargin of safety = ($527.75 \u2212 $411.79) / $527.75 = 22.0% \u2192 ADEQUATE (15\u201330%)\n50% CI: $430\u2013$620 | 80% CI: $340\u2013$700 [Assumption - derived from scenario spread]\n\nBayesian update (prior \u2192 posterior):\n\u2022 Prior (base rate): 35% probability a serial acquirer outperforms the market.\n\u2022 Evidence (likelihood ratio): raised FY2026 guidance twice; FCF +11% H1; buyback at\n  $356 avg; organic 5-6% for two quarters; Deltek softness flagged.\n\u2022 Posterior: ~45% probability of outperformance. We incorporate this by keeping Base\n  at 50% and Bear above pure base-rate (25%).\n\n\nSECTION 4.5 \u2014 TIME-HORIZON ARBITRAGE\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\u2022 Market-implied perpetual revenue growth: ~2.5\u20133.0% (reverse DCF, Section 3).\n\u2022 My horizon: 3\u20135 years at 5.5\u20137.5% total revenue growth (guided organic 5-6% +\n  modest M&A).\n\u2022 Spread: ~3pts of growth for ~5 years. If correct, the market's implied horizon is\n  effectively zero growth / no M&A \u2014 a materially pessimistic embedded assumption.\n\u2022 BUT: the spread can close by de-rating, not re-rating. Roper at 18.5x forward\n  earnings could go to 16x (bear) or 25x (bull). The arbitrage exists only if the\n  organic-growth KPI holds at \u22655%.\n\nSECTION 5 \u2014 CONVICTION & POSITION SIZING\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nConviction score (15):\n1. Business quality: 3 \u2014 wide moat, stable 28% OPM, 30% FCF margins.\n2. Valuation attractiveness: 2 \u2014 22% MoS on probability-weighted IV; adequate,\n   not a deep value.\n3. Macro alignment: 1 \u2014 rates elevated/hawkish, late-cycle; headwind.\n4. Thesis clarity: 3 \u2014 KPIs measurable: organic growth, recurring revenue growth,\n   FCF/share, net debt/EBITDA, buyback pace.\n5. Execution traceability: 3 \u2014 12yr margin stability, guidance raised twice 2026,\n   $3.2B buyback demonstrates conviction.\nTOTAL: 12/15 \u2192 HIGH.\nAdjustments: Macro headwind \u22120.5 step; red-team logic gap (Section 9) \u22120.5 step.\nFinal conviction: HIGH (lower end).\n\nSizing: Conviction High \u2192 6\u20138% normal max. Macro headwind + leverage cap: \u22121pct.\nEngine 1 (no option cap). Final target weight: 5.0% (range 4\u20136%).\n\n\nSECTION 5.5 \u2014 BEHAVIORAL & POSITIONING\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\u2022 Short interest: 6.25% of float; 5.38M shares; days-to-cover 5.89; MoM \u22126.8%\n  [Actual - vendor]. Moderate short interest, declining. Squeeze fuel exists but\n  is not extreme.\n\u2022 Institutional ownership: 106.84% [Actual - vendor] (13F overcount typical);\n  insider 0.39% [Actual - vendor].\n\u2022 Insider activity: [Unconfirmed]; management signaled conviction via $3.2B buyback\n  [Actual - ainvest report on company actions], not via open-market purchases.\n\u2022 Options positioning: [Unconfirmed]. Given the 24% drawdown and put/call volume\n  [Unconfirmed], no reliable skew signal.\n\u2022 Sentiment: valuation reset from 25-30x to 18.5x; sell-side mean target $440.25\n  implies +7% [Estimate - vendor]; price near the middle of the 52-wk range\n  ($305.96\u2013$544.94). Sentiment is neutral-to-negative, which supports a contrarian\n  accumulation stance.\n\u2022 Crowding risk: 4/15 [Assumption] \u2014 not crowded after the drawdown; ETF/passive\n  ownership is a structural holder base, not marginal.\nCrowding adjustment: no reduction beyond Section 5 sizing.\n\nSECTION 6 \u2014 PORTFOLIO FIT\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\u2022 Roper's beta 0.737 [Aggregator] and defensive vertical software provide ballast\n  to a growth/cyclical-heavy portfolio.\n\u2022 Portfolio-level bear triggers (common with other positions): rising rates, credit\n  spread widening, AI-driven multiple compression in software. Position is\n  complement if the rest of the book is already rate-hedged.\n\u2022 Liquid approx. 5% total equity portfolio \u2192 expected loss in a 2022-style shock\n  (\u221235% peak-to-trough): ~1.75% of portfolio CVaR contribution [Estimate].\n\u2022 Fit score: GOOD as a quality/compounder sleeve; AVOID if portfolio already has\n  >15% exposure to mid-cap software ex-AI winners.\n\nSECTION 6.5 \u2014 KELLY SIZING\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\u2022 Estimated probability of reaching IV: 45% (Bayesian posterior).\n\u2022 Payoff ratio: upside to IV \u2248 $527.75, downside to bear \u2248 $339 (and to 52-wk low\n  $306 in tail). Average win = +$116; average loss = \u2212$73 (blended bear/tail).\n\u2022 Odds ratio b = 116/73 = 1.59.\n\u2022 Kelly fraction f* = p \u2212 (1\u2212p)/b = 0.45 \u2212 0.55/1.59 = 0.45 \u2212 0.346 = 10.4%.\n\u2022 Half-Kelly = 5.2%.\n\u2022 Layer 5 sizing (5%) vs Kelly (5.2%): gap = 0.2pp \u2192 well within the 20pp\n  re-analysis threshold. CONVERGED.\nFinal equity weight: 5.0%.\n\nSECTION 7 \u2014 RISKS & CATALYSTS\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nUpside catalysts:\n1. Organic growth re-accelerates to 6-7% (AI upsell, Deltek recovery) \u2014 the primary\n   KPI.\n2. Leverage paydown resumes (FCF $2.6B/yr); net debt/EBITDA back to <2.5x by 2027,\n   restoring M&A engine.\n3. Continued buybacks below $400 \u2014 every $1B at $380 removes ~2.6M shares (~2.7%).\n4. Rates fall 50-75bp; long-duration software re-rates 2-4 multiple points.\n\nDownside risks:\n1. Organic growth <3% for two quarters (AI disruption, budget cycles) \u2014 thesis broken.\n2. Net debt/EBITDA >4x if EBITDA dips or more debt-funded buybacks.\n3. M&A price inflation: CentralReach at 22x EBITDA sets a precedent for value\n   destruction.\n4. GAAP ROIC < WACC persists (5.7% vs 7.5%) \u2014 indicates goodwill-driven book value.\n5. Government/healthcare budget austerity hits Deltek and healthcare software.\n\nPre-mortem (mandatory): In 5 years, ROP is at $250 because: (1) organic growth fell\nfrom 5-6% to 2-3% as AI-native vertical tools replaced high-touch UI; (2) Roper had\nno dry powder (3.5x leverage) to acquire the disruptors; (3) the market de-rated\nquality compounders to 14x earnings. Early warning indicators: organic growth <3%\n2 quarters; recurring revenue growth <4%; renewal rates <85%; M&A deployment <$1B/yr;\nEBITDA margin <38%.\n\nSECTION 7.5 \u2014 CORRELATION-ADJUSTED RISK\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\u2022 Common bear triggers with the rest of a typical equity portfolio: a 100bp rise in\n  10Y yields, credit spread widening (M&A/leveraged model vulnerable), and software\n  multiple compression.\n\u2022 Tail correlation: ROP vs NASDAQ during risk-off: historically ~0.7 (2020, 2022)\n  [Estimate - not retrieved, assumed from beta and sector]; ROP will NOT decouple in\n  a real recession.\n\u2022 CVaR (95%, 1-year): \u221230% to \u221235% [Estimate], implying a tail price of ~$270\u2013290,\n  below the 52-wk low.\n\u2022 Portfolio-level: 5% position \u00d7 \u221232.5% CVaR = \u22121.6% portfolio impact in tail. Acceptable.\n\nSECTION 8 \u2014 MONITORING RULES\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nRe-evaluation triggers (any two within two quarters):\n\u2022 Organic revenue growth <3% (company-reported).\n\u2022 Recurring software revenue growth <5%.\n\u2022 Net debt/EBITDA >4.0x (company-reported).\n\u2022 Adjusted FCF margin ex-SBC <27%.\n\u2022 Buyback suspension or M&A guidance withdrawal.\n\u2022 Two consecutive quarters of adjusted DEPS guidance cuts.\nPositive update triggers (add to conviction/size):\n\u2022 Organic growth \u22656% for 2 quarters.\n\u2022 Deleveraging >$1.5B in 12 months.\n\u2022 Major AI-driven product wins disclosed on earnings calls.\n\nMonitoring cadence: quarterly earnings, monthly short interest, weekly price vs\n$380/$350 tranche levels.\n\nSECTION 9 \u2014 RED TEAM (ADVERSARIAL)\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nShort thesis (5-7 bullets):\n1. Organic growth has decelerated from ~8% to 5% and Deltek/government demand is\n   soft [Estimate 